  "notes": "The two short prayers said once both gifts lie on the corporal. They are kept in one document because neither accompanies a new offering: the host and the chalice have been offered, and these complete the act — the first asking that the offerers themselves be received, the second calling the Sanctifier to come and bless what already lies there. Witness handmissal-eo prints them in one paragraph with the chalice prayer; witness do separates them by rubrics, which is the shape followed here. SANCTIFICÁTOR at w027 is a standing divergence: Whitaker's has no such agent noun and reads the form as a future passive imperative of sanctifico, while Collatinus heads sanctificator and reads the vocative; the token's provenance records the disagreement. BÉNEDIC at w032 is one word; witness do prints a cross glyph inside it (béne + dic) and the witness header declares the stripping. TUO at w035 and SANCTO at w036 are dative, agreeing with nómini, not ablative — the same spelling serves both cases.",
